  {
    "title": "Effective value-count bounds for reciprocal linear recurrences via linear forms in logarithms",
    "description": "Consider two integer linear recurrences (a_n) and (b_m) whose characteristic polynomials are reverses of each other, each with a single dominant root of modulus greater than one. The number of common values a_n = b_m exceeding a threshold C is conjecturally at most two once C is large enough. Formalize the transcendence-theoretic step: derive an explicit lower bound for the linear form |n log alpha - m log beta| from Baker's theorem, combine it with the elementary upper bound coming from a_n = b_m, and conclude that only finitely many (n,m) survive with an effectively computable bound on max(n,m). This supplies the currently-hypothesized value bound feeding the proven index reduction.",
    "tags": ["NumberTheory", "Diophantine", "LinearRecurrence"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Sharpness of the constant two for ABC recurrence coincidences",
    "description": "The headline theorem asserts at most two large common values. Investigate whether two is attained and whether it is optimal across the family of generalized ABC polynomials. Construct explicit polynomial data P_1,...,P_{q-1} whose reciprocal recurrence pair achieves exactly two large coincidences, and prove a matching lower-bound construction. The key object is the S-unit equation attached to the coincidence a_n = b_m; classify when its solution set has cardinality exactly two.",
    "tags": ["NumberTheory", "Combinatorics", "SUnitEquations"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Monotone reduction for value coincidences of arbitrary strictly increasing sequences",
    "description": "The reduction proved here shows that for a strictly increasing integer sequence a, the set of indices n with a_n a common value above C is equinumerous to the set of common values above C. Generalize this to eventually-monotone sequences, to sequences over ordered rings, and to k-fold coincidences a_n = b_m = c_l. The key insight is that injectivity transports cardinality between index and value sets; determine the minimal monotonicity hypotheses under which the transport still holds and whether ncard can be replaced by measure or density for non-monotone sequences.",
    "tags": ["Combinatorics", "OrderTheory", "SetCardinality"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Formal reciprocal-root calculus for characteristic polynomials of recurrences",
    "description": "Building on the reverse-polynomial root correspondence, develop a reusable formal library relating a linear recurrence to its reciprocal recurrence: dominant-root inversion, multiplicity preservation on nonzero roots, the effect of reversal on the Binet expansion, and the induced map on solution spaces. The key insight is that coefficient reversal on polynomials is dual to root inversion on nonzero roots, an involution that lifts to an isomorphism of geometric-solution families. Prove that the reciprocal recurrence's solution space is carried isomorphically onto the original by the inversion map on the multiplicative group of nonzero roots.",
    "tags": ["Algebra", "Polynomials", "LinearRecurrence"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Coincidence of powers for multiplicatively independent bases and its recurrence analogue",
    "description": "The instance 3^n = 2^m has only the trivial solution n=m=0, proved by a parity argument. Generalize: for multiplicatively independent integers p, q the equation p^n = q^m has only n=m=0, and more generally the common values of the geometric sequences (p^n) and (q^m) form a finite set governed by gcd structure. The key insight is that multiplicative independence of the bases is the elementary shadow of the distinct-dominant-root hypothesis in the ABC problem. Extend to non-degenerate binary recurrences (a_n) and (b_m) with coprime dominant roots and prove finiteness of their coincidence set unconditionally where the roots are rational.",
